   {:report
    "# Comparison Report: microFIT Contract Version 2.0 vs Version 3.1.1\n\nPrepared: Comparison of the two supplied contract texts (microFIT Contract Version 2.0 — \"Document A\" — and Version 3.1.1 — \"Document B\").\n\nSummary\n- The two documents are substantially the same in structure, ordering and most wording. Key differences are concentrated in a few related areas: removal of explicit Domestic Content references/requirements, small administrative changes to notification processes (requirement to notify \"in the Prescribed Form\"), and changes to the scope and examples of information collection and disclosure (including an added disclosure rationale for public health/safety / emergency planning).\n- Most sections are identical or contain only minor wording edits. The differences below focus on the materially changed clauses and their practical impacts.\n\nDetailed findings\n1. Major change: Domestic Content requirement removed\n- Version 2.0 explicitly defines a \"Minimum Required Domestic Content Level\" (60%) in Part 1.B(5)(iv) and includes a specific representation that, for solar PV facilities, the Facility meets that Domestic Content Level (Part 2, Section 4.4.4 in V2.0). Version 3.1.1 omits the Minimum Required Domestic Content Level definition from Part 1 and omits the corresponding representation requiring the Facility to meet the domestic content threshold.\n- Impact: This is a substantive change to supplier compliance obligations and program eligibility-related representations. It reduces a supplier obligation present in V2.0 and eliminates an explicit contractual representation regarding domestic content.\n\n2. Important change: Information collection / disclosure revisions and added emergency/public-safety disclosure\n- The Contract language describing what data may be collected and shared by OPA/LDC/Government ministries has been edited.\n  - V2.0 explicitly lists items such as GST number and information to establish compliance with the Minimum Required Domestic Content Level among data that may be collected or verified.\n  - V3.1.1 removes references to GST number and to domestic content compliance in that list, and adds express language allowing disclosure of certain Facility details to other government ministries/agencies/institutions/organizations for public health and safety / emergency services planning and response.\n- Impact: The removal of domestic content and GST implies narrower specified data collection (but does not necessarily prohibit other lawful collections). The added explicit emergency/public-safety disclosure broadens the stated permitted disclosures and may increase the likelihood of broader data sharing (for emergency planning/response) compared with V2.0.\n\n3. Minor/administrative change: \"Prescribed Form\" for notice of changes\n- In the Covenants section (5.1.2), V2.0 required Supplier to \"notify OPA promptly of any change to the information contained in Part 1, Section A(11)\". V3.1.1 modifies that to require notice \"promptly in the Prescribed Form.\"\n- Impact: This introduces a procedural requirement that may affect how suppliers must submit notifications (format/contents). Practically, this raises a compliance/administrative formality and may require suppliers to use a specific form or process.\n\n4. Minor wording and reordering adjustments\n- A small reordering of sub-clause numbering occurs in Representations and Warranties around references to Renewable Energy Approval and (in V2.0) domestic content; this is consequential only insofar as it reflects the removal of the domestic content representation and reindexing of subsequent clauses.\n- Several other paragraphs contain near-identical wording; a few clarifications and expansions are present (for example, the V3.1.1 disclosure section includes an explicit sentence about emergency/public-safety disclosure that did not appear in V2.0).\n\nRecommendations for users/contract managers\n- If you rely on the Domestic Content requirement for eligibility or compliance verification, note that Version 3.1.1 removes that contractual requirement. Confirm whether program rules (outside the contract text) still impose domestic content conditions or whether the program authority intends to stop enforcing them.\n- Review and, if necessary, update compliance processes to accommodate the \"Prescribed Form\" requirement in V3.1.1 (obtain the prescribed form and confirm submission methods).\n- Review data-sharing/privacy implications of the added emergency/public-safety disclosure language in V3.1.1, and consider whether suppliers should be provided with further notice or consent forms reflecting that possible disclosure. Ensure suppliers understand what facility details might be shared and under what circumstances.\n- If this contract will be used as-is, make sure internal recordkeeping reflects the narrower list of explicitly enumerated data items in V3.1.1 and the expanded disclosure rationale to other agencies.\n\nConclusion\n- The two versions are largely identical in most operational and commercial terms. The most consequential differences remove an explicit domestic content obligation and change some data collection/disclosure language while introducing a procedural notification form requirement. Those differences should be reviewed for regulatory, compliance, procurement, and privacy implications.",
